<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calc-papers.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calc-papers-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calc-papers.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="749" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/property-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Insurance Estimate Review</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Insurance Estimate Review for PA Property Owners</h1><p class="lede">Before demanding appraisal, it helps to know exactly where the insurer&#x27;s estimate falls short. Our estimate review gives you that answer in writing.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Deliverable</dt><dd>Written review</dd></div><div><dt>Checks</dt><dd>Scope, price, depreciation</dd></div><div><dt>Useful for</dt><dd>Deciding next steps</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>A second look before a bigger step</h2>
<p>Most owners first see their claim through the insurer's estimate: dozens of pages of codes, quantities and unit prices. It is easy to sense the number is too low and hard to say why. An estimate review turns that feeling into specifics you can act on, whether that means asking the adjuster for a supplement, demanding appraisal or simply accepting the offer with confidence.</p>
<h2>What we check</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What we look for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Rooms, elevations or roof slopes left out; missing tear-out, prep or cleanup</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quantities</td><td>Measurements that do not match the property; waste factors that are too low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Unit costs that do not reflect your region or specialty trades such as slate or plaster</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Rates applied to labor, overly aggressive age assumptions, holdback handling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overhead and profit</td><td>Whether a general contractor's costs belong in a multi-trade repair</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code items</td><td>Required upgrades and whether the policy addresses them</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>How the review works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>You send the documents:</strong> the insurer's estimate, photos, the policy declarations and any contractor bids.</li>
<li><strong>We study the file</strong> and, where it adds value, inspect the property.</li>
<li><strong>We write up the findings</strong> item by item, with an estimated dollar range for the gap.</li>
<li><strong>We talk it through with you,</strong> including whether appraisal seems worthwhile.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-calculators/claim-gap-fee-projector/">claim gap fee projector</a> gives a rough sense of whether the difference justifies appraisal costs, and the <a href="/claim-terms-glossary/">claim terms glossary</a> explains the jargon you will see in any estimate.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">An illustrative example</b>Suppose, for example, an insurer prices an asphalt roof at a low waste factor, omits drip edge and starter course, and depreciates labor along with materials. Each item looks small. Together they can move a claim by a meaningful amount. That kind of pattern is exactly what a review is meant to surface.</div>
<h2>What a review is not</h2>
<p>We are appraisers. A review does not negotiate with the insurer on your behalf, which in Pennsylvania is work for a licensed public adjuster, and it does not give legal advice. If coverage has been denied, or if the claim may end up in litigation, a Pennsylvania attorney is the right next call.</p>
<h2>When to ask for one</h2>
<p>The best time is soon after you receive the insurer's estimate and before you sign anything describing the payment as final. Reviews also help after a supplement is denied, or when an appraisal demand is on the table and you want to know how strong your position is. Keep the suit-limitation clause in your policy in mind; many Pennsylvania policies set one year from the date of loss. To see how an estimate review leads into the panel stage, read our overview of the <a href="/appraisal-process-explained/">appraisal process</a>.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I need an estimate review before demanding appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It is not required, but it helps. A review shows where the disagreement lies and roughly how large it is, which makes the appraisal decision easier.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you review my contractor&#x27;s estimate as well?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Comparing the insurer&#x27;s and contractor&#x27;s estimates side by side often shows which differences are real and which come from scope or pricing assumptions.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will the insurer accept your review?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some adjusters revise their estimate after a well-supported review; others do not. If they will not, the review becomes a strong starting point for appraisal.</p></div></details></div>
